static.solution:
We denote the faces of the cube by:
\begin{array}{rclrclrcl} S_1 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, OABC \amp S_2 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, DGEF \amp S_3 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, ABGF \\ S_4 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, OCDE \amp S_5 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, BCDG \amp S_6 \amp =\amp \mathrm{Face}\, OAFE \end{array}
\bullet On S_1 {\mathrm{\Phi }}_1( x,y )=( x,y,0 ) and {\mathbf{n} }_1= \left\lt 0,0,-1 \right>. Thus, \mathbf{F} \left({\mathrm{\Phi }}_1( x,y )\right)\cdot {\mathbf{n} }_1= \left\lt 0,0, e^y \right>\cdot \left\lt 0,0,-1 \right> =- e^y \bullet On S_2 {\mathrm{\Phi }}_2( x,y )=( x,y,8 ) and {\mathbf{n} }_2= \left\lt 0,0,1 \right>. Thus, \mathbf{F} \left({\mathrm{\Phi }}_2( x,y )\right)\cdot {\mathbf{n} }_2= \left\lt 0,0, e^{y+8} \right>\cdot \left\lt 0,0,1 \right> = e^{y+8} \bullet On any other surface S_i, 3\le i\le 6, we have \mathbf{F} \left({\mathrm{\Phi}}_1(x,y)\right)\cdot {\mathbf{n} }_i =0, because the z-component of {\mathbf{n} }_i is 0 and the x, y components of \mathbf{F} equal 0. Thus, \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_{S_1} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S}+\iint_{S_2} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S}= \int_0^{8} \int_0^{8} - e^y \,dx \,dy+\int_0^{8} \int_0^{8} e^{y+8} \,dx \,dy=\int_0^{8} \int_0^{8} \left(e^{y+8}-e^y \right) \,dx\,dy= {8}\int_0^{8} \left(e^{y+8}-e^y\right) \,dy= 8\int_0^{8} e^y (e^{8}-1) \,dy=8 \left(e^{8}-1\right) e^y \bigg|_0^{8}= 8\left(e^{8}-1\right)\left(e^{8}-1\right)=8\left(e^{8}-1\right)^2
